Note: `androidx.activity.result.ActivityResultLauncher<Unit>` with `launch(Unit)` — `ActivityResultLauncher<I>.launch(input: I)`. `ActivityResultLauncher<Unit>` is an interface with `launch(input: Unit)`. `ActivityResultLauncher<Unit> { launcher.launch(...) }` — SAM conversion: ActivityResultLauncher is a Java-style interface with a single method `launch(I)`. In Kotlin, an interface with a single abstract method can be SAM-converted only if declared `fun interface`. `androidx.activity.result.ActivityResultLauncher` is a Kotlin `abstract class`? Let me recall: `public interface ActivityResultLauncher<I>` — it's an interface in Java? It's written in Kotlin as:

```kotlin
public interface ActivityResultLauncher<I> {
    public fun launch(input: I)
    public fun unregister()
}
```
Two abstract methods → NOT a SAM interface → SAM conversion won't compile! I must implement it as an object:

```kotlin
return object : ActivityResultLauncher<Unit>() {  // if class
```
If it's an interface, `object : ActivityResultLauncher<Unit> { override fun launch(input: Unit) {...} }`.

Hmm, but `registerForActivityResult` returns `ActivityResultLauncher<I>`. A cleaner approach: return a small custom interface of my own:

```kotlin
fun interface Launcher { fun launch() }
fun register(activity: Activity, onResult: (Boolean) -> Unit): Launcher = Launcher { ... }
```

That avoids all ActivityResultLauncher typing issues. Much cleaner. Let me restructure with my own `fun interface`.

Also `ActivityResultContracts.StartActivityForResult()` requires `androidx.activity` — the app depends on androidx.activity.compose which brings activity-ktx. Good.

Let me rewrite OverlayPermission.kt with a `fun interface ActionLauncher`.

