Now for maps. Using `mapOf("a" to 'а', ...)`.

Careful with Kotlin type inference on `mapOf("a" to 'а')` → Map<String, Char>. Good.

Let me write all the pieces now.

For regex patterns, I'll compile once into `private val RE_X = Pattern.compile(...)` and use `.matcher(str)`.

Now, `fixLookalikesPerWord`:

```kotlin
@JvmStatic
fun fixLookalikesPerWord(text: String): String {
    if (text.isEmpty()) return text
    val m = WORD_RE.matcher(text)
    if (!m.find()) return text
    val sb = StringBuilder(text.length)
    var last = 0
    do {
        sb.append(text, last, m.start())
        sb.append(fixWord(m.group(0)))
        last = m.end()
    } while (m.find())
    sb.append(text, last, text.length)
    return sb.toString()
}
```
Careful: `m.find()` in the do-while — I call `m.find()` once in the `if (!m.find())` then again in `while (m.find())`. That's the standard pattern: the first find positions at the first match, then the loop continues. Correct.

`fixWord(word: String): String` — private.

```kotlin
private fun fixWord(word: String): String {
    val runs = letterRuns(word)
    if (runs.size >= 2 && hasAlphabetSwitch(runs)) return splitMixedRuns(runs)

    var cyrUnique = 0
    var latUnique = 0
    for (c in word) {
        if (CYR_UNIQUE.contains(c)) cyrUnique++
        if (LAT_UNIQUE.contains(c)) latUnique++
    }
    if (cyrUnique == 0 && latUnique == 0) {
        var cyrAll = 0; var latAll = 0
        for (c in word) {
            if (isCyr(c)) cyrAll++
            if (isAsciiLetter(c)) latAll++
        }
        if (cyrAll > latAll) return mapChars(mapChars(word, LAT_TO_CYR), UKR_TO_RUS)
        if (latAll > cyrAll) return mapChars(word, CYR_TO_LAT)
        return word
    }
    if (cyrUnique > latUnique) return mapChars(mapChars(word, LAT_TO_CYR), UKR_TO_RUS)
    if (latUnique > cyrUnique) return mapChars(word, CYR_TO_LAT)
    return word
}
```

Note: Python's `sum(1 for c in word if c in _CYR_UNIQUE)` — CYR_UNIQUE is a set of single chars. `LAT_UNIQUE` likewise.

letterRuns:
```kotlin
private fun letterRuns(word: String): List<String> {
    val out = ArrayList<String>(4)
    val m = RUN_RE.matcher(word)
    while (m.find()) {
        val s = m.group(0)
        if (isLatRun(s) || isCyrRun(s)) out.add(s)
    }
    return out
}
```

hasAlphabetSwitch:
```kotlin
private fun hasAlphabetSwitch(runs: List<String>): Boolean {
    for (i in 0 until runs.size - 1) {
        val a = runs[i]; val b = runs[i + 1]
        if (a.length >= 2 && b.length >= 2 &&
            ((isCyrRun(a) && isLatRun(b)) || (isLatRun(a) && isCyrRun(b)))) return true
    }
    return false
}
```

splitMixedRuns:
```kotlin
private fun splitMixedRuns(runs: List<String>): String {
    val out = StringBuilder()
    var prevLetters = ""
    var prevWasAny = false  // res non-empty
    for (r in runs) {
        val chunk = if (isCyrRun(r) && r.length >= 2) mapChars(r, UKR_TO_RUS) else r
        val insertSpace = prevWasAny && r.length >= 2 &&
            ((isLatRun(r) && isCyrRun(prevLetters)) || (isCyrRun(r) && isLatRun(prevLetters)))
        if (insertSpace) out.append(' ')
        out.append(chunk)
        prevWasAny = true
        if (isLatRun(r) || isCyrRun(r)) prevLetters = r
        else if (chunk.isBlank()) prevLetters = ""
    }
    return out.toString()
}
```
Wait, `chunk.strip() == ""` in Python uses the transformed chunk; `chunk.isBlank()` in Kotlin is not the same as Python's `.strip() == ""` for non-breaking space? Kotlin's `isBlank()` uses `Char.isWhitespace()`, Python's `str.strip()` strips chars where `str.isspace()` is true. Both are Unicode whitespace. Close enough. Actually in this path chunks are either letter runs or (unreachable) non-letter runs. Fine.

Also `prevWasAny` = `res` non-empty in Python, and note the `res.append(" ")` happens BEFORE `res.append(chunk)`, so the condition `res and ...` is evaluated before the space is appended. My `prevWasAny` tracks whether anything was appended (including spaces). Since a space is only appended together with a chunk, and after the first iteration prevWasAny is always true... wait: if res is non-empty because a " " was appended but no chunk? Impossible — both happen in the same iteration. So `res non-empty` ⟺ `prevWasAny`. Correct.

Hmm, but careful: `isLatRun(r)` uses the ORIGINAL r while Python uses `chunk` (post-UKR mapping) for `_is_lat_run(chunk)` and `_is_cyr_run(chunk)` in the space condition, and `chunk` for prev_letters update too. Does UKR_TO_RUS mapping change the alphabet class? UKR_TO_RUS maps І→И, Ї→Ы, Є→Е — all Cyrillic to Cyrillic. So isCyrRun(chunk) == isCyrRun(r) and isLatRun(chunk) == isLatRun(r) (a Cyrillic run can't become Latin). And the length is unchanged. So using `r` is equivalent. Good.

Now `joinLineHyphens`:
```kotlin
private val HYPHEN_RE = Pattern.compile("(\\p{L})-[ \\t]*\\n[ \\t]*(\\p{L})")
@JvmStatic
fun joinLineHyphens(text: String): String {
    if (text.isEmpty()) return text
    val m = HYPHEN_RE.matcher(text)
    if (!m.find()) return text
    val sb = StringBuilder(text.length)
    var last = 0
    do {
        sb.append(text, last, m.start(1))
        sb.append(text, m.start(2), m.end(2))
        last = m.end()
    } while (m.find())
    sb.append(text, last, text.length)
    return sb.toString()
}
```
Note: Python applies the same sub twice (a no-op the second time, most likely) — single pass is equivalent.

Hmm, is it exactly a no-op? After the first sub, could a new match appear? Pattern `(\w)-\s*\n\s*(\w)`. Removing a `\w` and `-` from between them: e.g. "а-\nб-в" — first match "а-\nб" → "аб-в" wait no: group2 is `б`, replaced result is "аб-в" hmm then a new pattern needs `\w-\s*\n\s*\w`. The result "аб-в" has no \n. Generally the substitution removes the \n, so no new \n-based matches can be created... unless the replacement creates a new \w-...\n adjacency. The removed parts are exactly `-\s*\n\s*` between two word chars. After removal, text is `X Y` adjacent. Could the text after the match end with a `-` and then something? The following text after group2 starts with whatever came after `б`. If the original was "а-\nб-\nв", the first pass matches "а-\nб" and produces "аб-\nв" — but Python's `re.sub` continues scanning AFTER the match end (not re-scanning the replacement), so it finds the next match starting at the char after "б" → "-\nв" needs `(\w)-` — the `\w` before the `-` was consumed by group 2 of the first match, so no new match. So the second pass is a no-op there too. But there could be a case: text = "а-\nб" + "\n" + ... nah. It's safe: applying it once is equivalent, since the first pass already covers all non-overlapping matches and the second pass can only match new patterns that skip the `\n` we just removed — impossible because the pattern requires a `\n`, and the only `\n` between the two word chars was removed. Could a match straddle the removal point? E.g. text = "a-  \n  b" → after removal "ab". A second-pass match would need `\w-\s*\n\s*\w` — none. I'm confident: single pass is equivalent. Deviation noted.

Now `normalizeWhitespace` uses several patterns. Let me define them.

Then `normalizeNumbers` with `\b\d+[.,]\d*0+\b`.

Python's `\b` with Unicode semantics vs Java's `\b` (UNICODE_CHARACTER_CLASS not set by default → Java `\b` is `\w` based on the default `\w` = `[a-zA-Z_0-9]`). Hmm! This is a real difference: In Python, `\b` is Unicode-aware by default for str patterns: `\w` matches Unicode letters/digits/underscore. In Java without UNICODE_CHARACTER_CLASS, `\d` = `[0-9]`, `\w` = `[a-zA-Z_0-9]`.

Consider "цена1.500" — Python: `\w` includes Cyrillic, so between "а" and "1" there's no word boundary... wait, both are word chars, so no boundary; the match `\b\d+...` would need a boundary before "1" — since "а" and "1" are both \w in Python, no boundary → no match. In Java, "а" is not `\w`, so there IS a boundary before "1" → match → "цена1.5". That's a behavioral difference!

To match Python, I should enable UNICODE_CHARACTER_CLASS: `Pattern.UNICODE_CHARACTER_CLASS` makes `\w` match `[\p{Alpha}\p{gc=Mn}\p{gc=Me}\p{gc=Mc}\p{Digit}\p{gc=Pc}\p{IsJoin_Control}]` — close to Python's `\w` (Python's \w = alphanumerics + underscore, i.e. `str.isalnum() or c == '_'`). And `\d` under UNICODE_CHARACTER_CLASS matches Unicode digits (Nd category), while Python's `\d` for str = Unicode Nd too. Hmm, but Python's `\d` is Unicode-aware by default: yes, `re.match(r'\d', '٣')` matches (Arabic-Indic digit). And in `\b\d+[.,]\d*0+\b` with Unicode, a match like "٣.٠٠" would work in Python but in Java with UCHARCLASS, `\d` = Nd → also matches. And `0` in the pattern is a literal ASCII '0', same in both.

So using `Pattern.UNICODE_CHARACTER_CLASS` for the numbers pattern gives closest parity. But it changes `\d` to Unicode digits — which matches Python. However, `_clean_number`'s check `'.' not in s and ',' not in s` is unaffected.

Hmm, but there's a subtlety: with UCHARCLASS, `\b` treats Cyrillic letters as word chars, matching Python. I'll use `Pattern.UNICODE_CHARACTER_CLASS` for the normalize_numbers pattern. Good catch.

Similarly, `join_line_hyphens` uses `\w` — Python's `\w` is Unicode. My `\\p{L}` is close (letters only; Python's \w also includes digits and underscore). Let me use `[\p{L}\p{N}_]` for full parity: `Pattern.compile("([\\p{L}\\p{N}_])-[\\s\u00A0]*\\n[\\s\u00A0]*(...)", UNICODE_CHARACTER_CLASS)`. Simpler: with UNICODE_CHARACTER_CLASS, `\w` works in Java exactly as `[\p{Alpha}\p{gc=Mn}...`. So I can write the pattern as `(\\w)-[ \t]*\n[ \t]*(\\w)` with UNICODE_CHARACTER_CLASS and `\s` also becomes Unicode-aware (Java's `\s` = `[ \t\n\x0B\f\r]`; with UCHARCLASS it becomes `\p{IsWhite_Space}` which includes NBSP). Python's `\s` for str = Unicode whitespace including \xa0, \x1c-\x1f, \x85. So using `Pattern.UNICODE_CHARACTER_CLASS` + `\s` + `\w` gives near-exact parity! Excellent. I'll use UCHARCLASS for all patterns that use `\w`, `\s`, `\d`.

Which patterns use them?
- join_line_hyphens: `(\w)-\s*\n\s*(\w)` → UCHARCLASS.
- normalize_whitespace: `[ \t]+` (no classes), ` *\n *` (no), `\.{4,}` (no), `\.\.\.` (no), `\s+([,.!?;:])` → UCHARCLASS, `([(\[«])\s+` → UCHARCLASS.
- normalize_numbers: `\b\d+[.,]\d*0+\b` → UCHARCLASS.
- looksLikeDictionaryRamp split: `[\s,.\-!?;:...]+` → UCHARCLASS (Python's `\s` Unicode).
- filter_garbage_tokens: uses explicit classes, no.
- whitespace splitting: use `Pattern.compile("\\s+", UNICODE_CHARACTER_CLASS)`.

Note: with UCHARCLASS, `\s` in Java = `\p{IsWhite_Space}` = `[\t\n\x0B\f\r \u0085\u00A0\u1680\u2000-\u200A\u2028\u2029\u202F\u205F\u3000]`. Python's `\s` for str matches `[ \t\n\r\f\v\x1c-\x1f\x85\xa0\u1680\u2000-\u200a\u2028\u2029\u202f\u205f\u3000]` — nearly identical (differences: \x1c-\x1f in Python vs not in Java's IsWhite_Space; \u180e in neither now). Close enough.

So define a helper: `private fun pattern(regex: String): Pattern = Pattern.compile(regex, Pattern.UNICODE_CHARACTER_CLASS)`.

Now for `\b` with UCHARCLASS — Java's `\b` uses `\w` under the flag. Good.

Now let me write `normalizeWhitespace`:

```kotlin
@JvmStatic
fun normalizeWhitespace(text: String): String {
    if (text.isEmpty()) return text
    var out = SPACES_RUN.matcher(text).replaceAll(" ")          // [ \t]+ -> " "
    out = AROUND_NL.matcher(out).replaceAll("\n")               // " *\n *" -> "\n"
    out = FOUR_DOTS.matcher(out).replaceAll("…")                // \.{4,} -> …
    out = THREE_DOTS.matcher(out).replaceAll("…")               // \.\.\. -> …
    out = stripSpaceBeforePunct(out)                            // \s+([,.!?;:]) -> $1
    out = stripSpaceAfterOpen(out)                              // ([\(\[«])\s+ -> $1
    return out.split('\n').joinToString("\n") { it.trim() }
}
```

`Matcher.replaceAll(String)` — careful, the replacement string is subject to `$` and `\` processing. "$1" and "\n" are fine. `"\n"` as a Java string is an actual newline character, no escape processing needed. Good.

`stripSpaceBeforePunct`: use the appendReplacement loop because `replaceAll` with a capture group is fine: `replaceAll("\$1")` — in Kotlin, `"\$1"` is the literal `$1`. That works with Matcher.replaceAll. Simpler. But `replaceAll` with a group ref requires the pattern to have the group. Fine.

So: `PUNCT_SPACE.matcher(out).replaceAll("\$1")`. And `OPEN_BRACKET_SPACE.matcher(out).replaceAll("\$1")`. Hmm, but careful: for the second pattern, `([(\[«])\s+` → the `\s+` might include the `\n`, so `(\n` would become `(\n` and the newline removed! E.g. "(\nтекст" → "(\nтекст"? No: `[\(\[«]\s+` matches "(\n" and replaces with "(" → the newline is DELETED, joining lines. That's the Python behavior too (Python's sub does the same). Faithful port. OK, same.

Careful with Kotlin string escaping: `"([\\(«])\\s+"` — in a Kotlin string, `\(` is not a valid escape! I must write `"([(\\[«])\\s+"` or use triple-quoted string `"""([(\[«])\s+"""`. I'll use raw strings (""" ... """) for regexes to avoid double escaping. Nice and readable. But careful: `$` in a raw string needs escaping if followed by an identifier char or `{`. The patterns don't contain `$`. Good.

Now `filterGarbageTokens`:
```kotlin
@JvmStatic
fun filterGarbageTokens(text: String): String {
    if (text.isEmpty()) return text
    val sb = StringBuilder(text.length)
    var first = true
    for (line in text.split('\n')) {
        val kept = ArrayList<String>()
        for (w in splitWhitespace(line)) {
            when {
                CYR_RE.matcher(w).find() -> kept.add(w)
                LAT_RE.matcher(w).find() -> kept.add(w)
                w.all { PUNCT_CHARS.contains(it) || it in '0'..'9' } -> kept.add(w)
                else -> Log.d(TAG, "[Cleaner] Garbage token removed: '$w'")
            }
        }
        if (kept.isNotEmpty()) {
            if (!first) sb.append('\n')
            first = false
            sb.append(kept.joinToString(" "))
        }
    }
    return sb.toString()
}
```
WHITELIST = set(".,!?;:-'\"()—–…/ \n") — includes space and \n, but tokens from split() never contain whitespace. Keep them anyway for parity.

Note: the Python joins lines with "\n" for the kept lines only, dropping empty ones. My `first` flag handles that.

`looksLikeDictionaryRamp`:
```kotlin
@JvmStatic
fun looksLikeDictionaryRamp(text: String): Boolean {
    if (text.length < 10) return false
    for (word in RAMP_SPLIT.split(text)) {
        if (word.length < 8) continue
        var ascending = 1
        for (i in 1 until word.length) {
            if (word[i] > word[i - 1]) { ascending++; if (ascending >= 10) return true }
            else ascending = 1
        }
    }
    return false
}
```
`word[i] > word[i-1]` compares Chars → same as ord comparison. 

`split` with a `Pattern`: `Pattern.split(CharSequence)` returns Array<String>. With limit 0, trailing empty strings are removed; interior empties kept but they have length < 8 → skipped. Good.

Now `cyrillicFitness`:
```kotlin
@JvmStatic
fun cyrillicFitness(text: String): Double {
    var alpha = 0; var cyr = 0
    for (c in text) {
        if (!isAlphaChar(c)) continue
        alpha++
        if (isCyrLower(c) || c == 'ё' || c == 'Ё') cyr++
    }
    if (alpha == 0) return 0.0
    return cyr.toDouble() / alpha
}
```
where `isCyrLower(c)` = c in 'а'..'я' (U+0430..U+044F), matching Python's `"а" <= c.lower() <= "я"`. Note 'ё' (U+0451) is inside that range in Unicode, and Python's `c.lower()` for 'ё' is 'ё' which is in the range → counted. Same. And 'Ё' → 'ё' → in range → counted. Same.

`isAlphaChar(c)` = ASCII letter or Cyrillic letter (0x0400..0x052F). Deviation documented.

Return type: Python returns float. Kotlin Double. Or Float to match the TFLite usage (fitness compared with 0.5). I'll return Double to mirror Python.

`latinFitness` similar with ASCII letters.

`reattachLeadingPunct`:
```kotlin
@JvmStatic
fun reattachLeadingPunct(text: String): String {
    val out = ArrayList<String>()
    for (tok in text.split(' ')) {
        var i = 0
        while (i < tok.length && LEAD_PUNCT.contains(tok[i])) i++
        val last = if (out.isEmpty()) null else out[out.size - 1]
        if (i > 0 && i < tok.length && last != null && last.isNotEmpty() &&
            !LEAD_PUNCT.contains(last[last.length - 1]) && isAlnum(last[last.length - 1])) {
            out[out.size - 1] = last + tok.substring(0, i)
            val rest = tok.substring(i)
            if (rest.isNotEmpty()) out.add(rest)
        } else {
            out.add(tok)
        }
    }
    return out.filter { it.isNotEmpty() }.joinToString(" ")
}
```
`isAlnum` = ASCII alnum or Cyrillic (deviation from Python's isalnum; the last char comes from OCR text). I'll write `isAlnumChar(c) = (c in '0'..'9') || isAlphaChar(c)`.

`isAcceptableCyrillicText`: straightforward with regex matches.

`applyKnownCorrections`:
```kotlin
@JvmStatic
fun applyKnownCorrections(text: String): String {
    if (text.isEmpty()) return text
    val m = LAT_RUN_RE.matcher(text)
    if (!m.find()) return text
    val sb = StringBuilder(text.length)
    var last = 0
    do {
        sb.append(text, last, m.start())
        val w = m.group(0)
        sb.append(KNOWN_CORRECTIONS[w.lowercase()] ?: w)
        last = m.end()
    } while (m.find())
    sb.append(text, last, text.length)
    return sb.toString()
}
```

`normalizeLocalCyrillicCaption`:
```kotlin
@JvmStatic
fun normalizeLocalCyrillicCaption(text: String): String {
    if (text.isEmpty()) return text
    var t = joinLineHyphens(text)
    t = fixLookalikesPerWord(t)
    t = restoreKnownWords(t)
    t = correctVisualConfusions(t)
    return reattachLeadingPunct(t)
}
```

`fullCleanPipeline`:
```kotlin
@JvmStatic @JvmOverloads
fun fullCleanPipeline(text: String, engineType: String = ENGINE_TFLITE): String {
    if (text.isEmpty()) return text
    var t = normalizeWhitespace(text)
    t = fixLookalikesPerWord(t)
    t = joinLineHyphens(t)
    if (engineType in CYRILLIC_ENGINES) t = restoreKnownWords(t)
    t = filterGarbageTokens(t)
    t = applyKnownCorrections(t)
    t = normalizeNumbers(t)
    t = normalizeWhitespace(t)
    if (looksLikeDictionaryRamp(t)) Log.w(TAG, "[Cleaner] Dictionary ramp detected, text may be garbage: ${t.take(80)}")
    return t
}
```

`restoreKnownWords`:
```kotlin
@JvmStatic
fun restoreKnownWords(text: String): String {
    if (text.isEmpty()) return text
    val result = ArrayList<String>()
    for (word in splitWhitespace(text)) {
        if (word.length < 6 || !CYR_WORD_RE.matcher(word).matches()) { result.add(word); continue }
        val low = lowercaseAscii(word)
        if (low in KNOWN_WORDS) { result.add(word); continue }
        val split = dpSplitOnKnown(low, 20)
        if (split != null && split.size > 1 && split.all { it in KNOWN_WORDS }) {
            val restored = ArrayList<String>(split.size)
            var idx = 0
            for (part in split) {
                val orig = word.substring(idx, idx + part.length)
                restored.add(
                    when {
                        orig.isNotEmpty() && isUpperAll(orig) -> toUpper(part)
                        orig.isNotEmpty() && isUpperChar(orig[0]) -> capitalize(part)
                        else -> part
                    })
                idx += part.length
            }
            result.add(restored.joinToString(" "))
        } else {
            result.add(word)
        }
    }
    return result.joinToString(" ")
}
```

Careful: `orig_slice.isupper()` in Python: False if empty. orig can't be empty (part non-empty). `orig[0].isupper()`: safe.

`isUpperAll(s)` = `s.any { isUpperChar(it) } && s.none { isLowerChar(it) }`.

`toUpper(part)`: uppercase Cyrillic/ASCII. `part.uppercaseAscii()` won't handle Cyrillic. Write `toUpperMap(s) = s.map { upperCyr(it) }` → but note uppercase of 'ё' is 'Ё' (U+0451 - 32 = U+0431 = 'я'!). WRONG! ё (U+0451) - 32 = U+0431 = 'я'. The uppercase of ё is Ё (U+0401), which is - 80. So I must special-case 'ё' → 'Ё'. Similarly lowercase: 'Ё' - 80 = 'ё'.

Since _KNOWN_WORDS contains "всё", "её", "ещё", "чём" with ё, this matters. Let me define:

```kotlin
private fun upperCyr(c: Char): Char = when {
    c == 'ё' -> 'Ё'
    c == 'Ё' -> c
    c in 'а'..'я' -> c - 32
    c in 'a'..'z' -> c - 32
    else -> c
}
private fun lowerCyr(c: Char): Char = when {
    c == 'Ё' -> 'ё'
    c == 'ё' -> c
    c in 'А'..'Я' -> c + 32
    c in 'A'..'Z' -> c + 32
    else -> c
